    "abstract": [
      {
        "source": "grobid_tei",
        "value": "Attackers can access sensitive information of programs by exploiting the side-effects of speculatively-executed instructions using Spectre attacks. To mitigate these attacks, popular compilers deployed a wide range of countermeasures whose security, however, has not been ascertained: while some are believed to be secure, others are known to be insecure and result in vulnerable programs. This paper develops formal foundations for reasoning about the security of these defenses. For this, it proposes a framework of secure compilation criteria that characterise when compilers produce code resistant against Spectre v1 attacks. With this framework, this paper performs a comprehensive security analysis of countermeasures against Spectre v1 attacks implemented in major compilers, deriving the first security proofs of said countermeasures. This paper uses a blue, sans-serif font for elements of the source language and an orange, bold font for elements of the target language. Elements common to all languages are typeset in a black, italic font (to avoid repetitions). For a better experience, please print or view this in colour [48]."
      },
